\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Intensity of a univariate Hawkes process with \(\mu =0.5\), \(\alpha =0.8\) and \(\beta =2\), for three events (ticks on the axis). Each event adds a jump that decays with half-life \(\ln 2/2\approx 0.35\)~s. The dots are the intensities at the events, the terms that the likelihood rewards; the shaded area is the compensator, the term it penalizes.}}{18}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Cluster representation of a Hawkes process. Only the projection of the events on the time axis is observed; which event triggered which is hidden. With \(g=0.5\), each immigrant brings on average a cluster of \(1/(1-0.5)=2\) events.}}{18}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces The symmetric bivariate Hawkes process as a diagram of event types. Loops are self-excitation, arrows between types are cross-excitation; the same four parameters \((\mu ,\alpha _{\mathrm {self}},\alpha _{\mathrm {cross}},\beta )\) apply to both types. \(\alpha _{\mathrm {self}}>\alpha _{\mathrm {cross}}\) describes persistence of direction, as found in the crypto data of this work; \(\alpha _{\mathrm {cross}}>\alpha _{\mathrm {self}}\) describes reversion, as at the equity fixed point of \citeonline {lee2023}.}}{19}{figure.caption.10}%
