\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Test MSE by parameter under the 1,000-event protocol. The network error bars represent one standard deviation across seeds.}}{39}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Macro MSE by regime of the branching ratio. The network error bars represent one standard deviation across seeds.}}{39}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces Cost per path recorded during the final run, on a logarithmic scale. For the MLE, wall-clock time with four processes competing with training on the GPU; for the networks, median latency at batch size one on MPS. A later controlled benchmark measured about 10~ms per path for the MLE on a single core and should be preferred to this figure.}}{40}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces True versus estimated $\beta $ (3,000 test paths, logarithmic scale). The networks shrink the estimates toward the center of the prior; the MLE is unbiased over most of the domain but produces extreme values when $g$ is low.}}{41}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.5}{\ignorespaces MLE estimates on the real calibration windows (points) and support of the prior used to generate the training data (rectangles).}}{42}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.6}{\ignorespaces Left: Q--Q plot of the pooled time-rescaled residuals against $\mathrm {Exp}(1)$. Right: rejection rates on real data and in the two simulated controls; the dotted line is the nominal 5\%.}}{45}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.7}{\ignorespaces Left: median realized volatility per $\sqrt {\mathrm {s}}$ as a function of the sampling interval (signature plot) and the median Hawkes level. Right: Hawkes against realized volatility ($\Delta =10$~s) per window.}}{47}{figure.caption.38}%
